// This asserted *"'error_message' is a builtin function and cannot be redefined"* until 2026-10-18,
// when a program's function was allowed to shadow a builtin -- a `use`d module could always do it,
// and the main file could not, which was an asymmetry rather than a rule. So it changed sides: the
// declaration is legal, and a bare call in this file reaches the program's one.
//
// The builtin is not lost to the library that uses it: the prelude's own calls do not see a
// program's shadow, which is what keeps lib/prelude.zm type-checking when `len` is shadowed.
fn error_message(x: int) -> string {
    return "mine"
}

fn main() {
    print(error_message(7))
}
